  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/cbccf40aca753c4c23cc2b159697deca70b45844d2cbf458c9a04f6dc6d3026f.png)\n\nThe phase line of a differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the slope field of the equation?",
  "worked_solution": "From the phase line, we can deduce the following:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=0.$ Therefore, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along these lines.\n\n- $f(y)<0$ for $y<-2.$ Therefore, the slopes of the slope field are negative in this region.\n\n- $f(y)>0$ for $-2<y<0$ or $y>0.$ Therefore, the slopes of the slope field are positive in these regions.\n\nThe only slope field that satisfies the above conditions is the following.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/64d0e59ba56d8c2a582cc80d267c258681f26badf2b77fddb79c3ff17bdd7249.png)",
